WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.640
A useful auditory-training plan specifies the skill, the stimuli, the activity type, and the difficulty.

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:11.100
Formal work is structured, often one-to-one.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.660
Informal work occurs within daily routines.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.760
Both need an observable target.

00:00:17.635 --> 00:00:28.115
You can reduce difficulty by supplying a small known response set, choosing more distinct sounds, improving the listening conditions, adding familiar context, or structuring the activity.

00:00:28.975 --> 00:00:32.335
Change demands deliberately and document them.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.720
The goal is a meaningful challenge without frustration.

00:00:36.665 --> 00:00:42.985
When writing an objective, define the child's response, the conditions, the success criterion, and the time frame.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:52.520
For example, specify picture identification in quiet, the device and distance, whether cues are allowed, the number of correct trials, and the deadline.

00:00:53.365 --> 00:00:56.725
Those values come from the child's baseline and your plan.

00:00:57.415 --> 00:01:03.175
A cued audiovisual response does not demonstrate an independent auditory-only target.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:09.240
Keep the child's outcome separate from clinician responsibilities such as checking the device.

